  "concerns": [
    {
      "id": "def-four-dini-derivatives-at-a-point",
      "concern": "Its final sentence says that whenever the usual finite derivative exists it is the common value of “all four” Dini derivatives. Under the published relative-domain derivative convention, f(x)=x on [0,1] has f'(0)=1, but the left Dini derivatives at 0 are not well posed. The sentence needs an interior-point hypothesis or “all well-posed” replacement.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "rem-dini-derivatives-specialise-the-published-derivative",
      "concern": "It repeats the endpoint error: “that happens exactly when the upper and lower right and left Dini derivatives all agree.” A finite derivative at an interval endpoint can exist although the Dini derivatives on the absent side are undefined.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-one-sided-hardy-littlewood-inequality-for-continuous-monotone-functions",
      "concern": "Step 2.1 says “Reflecting H across the midpoint ... turns this into the right-hand rising-sun situation.” With H(x)=Rx-F(x), reflection alone produces the opposite inequality; one must also negate the reflected function (or state the corresponding falling-sun argument). The claimed inequality is standard, but this step does not establish it as written.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-right-continuous-increasing-functions-split-as-absolutely-continuous-plus-jump-plus-singular-continuous",
      "concern": "The statement gives F only on [a,b], but step 1.1 invokes “the Lebesgue-Stieltjes measure of F”; the cited Stieltjes correspondence and interval-formula theorem require a right-continuous nondecreasing function on R. The proof needs to specify the constant extension outside [a,b] and then verify the resulting finite measure and normalization.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-monotone-functions-are-differentiable-almost-everywhere-via-lebesgue-stieltjes-measures",
      "concern": "Step 2.1 similarly says “Let μ be the Lebesgue-Stieltjes measure of C_F” although C_F is defined only on [a,b], while its cited Stieltjes interface is for functions on R. A stated constant extension would make the intended measure argument type-correct.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "cor-second-borel-cantelli-lemma-under-pairwise-independence",
      "concern": "The cited frequency law is stated for sequences indexed n>=1 and sums from 1, but [L1] directly asserts its conclusion for the present sequence indexed from 0 and sums from 0. Removing/reindexing A_0 makes this valid, but that reduction is absent.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-gibbs-overshoot-at-a-piecewise-c-one-jump",
      "concern": "Step 1.2 claims that h=f-g is continuous after g is made linear on [δ,1-δ]. If f has another jump in that middle interval, h has that same jump, so the quoted claim and the uniform-convergence use of [F2] in step 2.1 are false. The theorem permits such piecewise C^1 functions; a valid proof must use a suitable localization argument or a construction that retains the other jumps. Also, the displayed change of variables in step 1.1 omits a factor 1/π.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    }
  ],
